# no provision for * and for n$ in the standard (the shell can fill them in); the references take them
'%*d\n' 3 5
'%.*s\n' 2 abc
'%1$d\n' 5
'%.*f\n' 2 1.5
'%*f\n' 6 1.5
'%1$f\n' 1.5
# hexadecimal escapes are not recognized in the format (APPLICATION USAGE); the references take \x41
'\x41'
# unspecified: a % with no conversion character after it, and \c in the format (only %b has it)
'a\cb'
'\%'
# the length modifiers h l ll L are not in the conversion specifications of the standard
'%Lf\n' 1.5
# the quote rule is for the integer conversions only (RATIONALE: "the special treatment of leading single-quote and double-quote applying only to integer conversions"); the references give the character's value to the floating-point ones too
"%f\n" "'A"
# a denormal in %a: ours is the C library's, normalized, as macOS writes it (macOS also reports the underflow); GNU has its own digits (long double).
'%a\n' 1e-310
# a NaN is written "[-]nan" (XBD File Format Notation, f and F; PCTS printf 37, 53, 76, 77 expect -nan for -NAN), so a negative NaN has its sign; macOS and GNU both leave it out
'%f\n' nan -nan +nan NAN -NAN nAn -nAn
'%F|%E|%G|%A\n' -nan -nan -nan -nan
'%e|%g|%a\n' -nan -nan -nan
'%6f|%-6f|%06f|%+f|% f|\n' -nan nan nan nan nan
'%10.3g|%-10.2e|%.0f|\n' -nAn -NAN nan
